# Polyhedral Earth: Structural Geometry, Solar Reception, and Cross-Scale Invariance

**Researcher and Author:** Christopher Macachor  
**Research Framework:** Macachor Research Corpus  
**Research Website:** https://earth.macachor.org/

### Abstract

This research examines polyhedral geometry as a structural model for investigating planetary form, solar reception, and geometric relationships across physical scales.

The central research direction is that continuous spherical representations and discrete polyhedral representations are not geometrically interchangeable. A polyhedron retains identifiable planar faces, edges, vertices, face normals, surface areas, and angular relationships. These properties provide a distinct geometric framework for examining orientation, projected surface area, directional exposure, and structural differentiation.

The planetary investigation focuses particularly on the June solstice and the Arctic regions of North America, Greenland, and northern Eurasia. These regions provide an important observational setting because locations north of the Arctic Circle experience continuous daylight during the appropriate seasonal interval. Regional temperature differences provide additional empirical measurements for investigating solar exposure, surface conditions, and environmental differentiation.

The mathematical investigation examines face-resolved solar incidence, projected area, orientation, and incident irradiance. It also distinguishes orbital distance, axial orientation, solar declination, geographic latitude, and local environmental conditions as separate measurable variables.

A further research direction investigates whether polyhedral geometric relationships can provide a consistent structural language for examining organization across scales, from atomic and molecular structures to biological organization and planetary geometry.

The Macachor framework distinguishes territory from cartographic representation. A geometric model is a representation whose validity must be evaluated through correspondence with observable reality, mathematical consistency, measurement, and empirical testing.

This work therefore presents polyhedral geometry as a research framework for comparative structural investigation, not as an experimentally established replacement for the conventional geodetic description of Earth.

The objective is to establish mathematically explicit, independently testable comparisons between geometric representations while preserving the distinction between observed measurements, derived mathematical relationships, structural hypotheses, and unresolved empirical questions.

**Governing principle:**

Territory is reality. Cartography is representation. Correspondence is the test.
